<!-- ===== SECTION 1: EXCEPTION BASICS ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="exception-basics">
  <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
  <h2>Understanding Exceptions</h2>

  <p>An exception is what Python throws when something goes wrong. Divide by zero. Try to open a file that doesn't exist. Access a dictionary key that wasn't defined. In beginner code, exceptions cause the program to crash with an error message. Professional code catches exceptions and handles them gracefully, continuing execution or failing safely.</p>

  <p>Python has a hierarchy of built-in exceptions, each representing a different kind of error. Understanding this hierarchy helps you catch the right exception and handle it appropriately.</p>

  <!-- ANALOGY BOX -->
  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>Think of exceptions like system alerts in a hospital. A low battery alert is handled differently from a critical patient alert. Python groups exceptions by severity and type. You might handle a file-not-found error (recoverable) one way, and an out-of-memory error (catastrophic) another way. Catching the right exception type means responding proportionately.</p>
  </div>

