<!-- ===== SECTION 3: CUSTOM EXCEPTIONS ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="custom-exceptions">
  <span class="section-label">Advanced Techniques</span>
  <h2>Creating Custom Exceptions</h2>

  <p>Built-in exceptions cover common scenarios, but domain-specific problems need custom exceptions. When a machine learning model receives invalid data, you might want to raise a ModelInputError. When a database connection fails, raise a DatabaseConnectionError. Custom exceptions make code more readable and let callers handle domain-specific problems.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Define custom exceptions</span>
<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="nm">ModelInputError</span>(<span class="nm">Exception</span>):
    <span class="st">"""Raised when model receives invalid input"""</span>
    <span class="kw">pass</span>

<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="nm">InvalidDataShape</span>(<span class="nm">ModelInputError</span>):
    <span class="st">"""Raised when input shape doesn't match model"""</span>
    <span class="kw">pass</span>

<span class="cm"># Use custom exceptions</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">process_model_input</span>(<span class="va">data</span>, <span class="va">expected_shape</span>):
    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">data</span>.<span class="va">shape</span> <span class="op">!=</span> <span class="va">expected_shape</span>:
        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="nm">InvalidDataShape</span>(
            <span class="st">f"Expected </span>{<span class="va">expected_shape</span>}<span class="st">, "</span>
            <span class="st">f"got </span>{<span class="va">data</span>.<span class="va">shape</span>}<span class="st">"</span>
        )
    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="va">data</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We define custom exception classes inheriting from Exception (or a parent like ModelInputError). This creates an exception hierarchy for our domain. When raising, include an informative message. Catch specific types or parent types — InvalidDataShape is caught first (more specific), then ModelInputError (parent class).</p>
  </div>

  <!-- WHY THIS MATTERS -->
  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Financial AI:</strong> Custom exceptions like InsufficientFundsError or FraudDetectionAlert let you write code that explicitly handles financial-specific problems. Logging and monitoring systems can react differently to fraud alerts vs system errors, protecting both users and the system.</p>
    <p><strong>In Life Sciences AI:</strong> Exceptions like DataIntegrityError or BiologicalConstraintViolation let you distinguish data quality issues (retry or ask for better data) from algorithmic failures (requires investigation). This separation is crucial in medical systems where the stakes are high.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- KEY TAKEAWAY -->
  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Create custom exceptions by inheriting from Exception</li>
      <li>Use exception hierarchies for different error categories</li>
      <li>Include descriptive messages when raising exceptions</li>
      <li>Custom exceptions improve code readability and debugging</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="section-divider">

<!-- ===== SECTION 4: DEBUGGING ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="debugging-tools">
  <span class="section-label">Best Practices</span>
  <h2>Debugging Strategies and Tools</h2>

  <p>When something breaks, you need to find out why. Python provides powerful debugging tools: the interactive debugger (pdb), strategic print statements, logging, and assertions. Professional developers use all of these, choosing the right tool for each situation.</p>

  <h3>Print-Based Debugging</h3>
  <p>Simple but effective. Strategic print statements show variable values, execution flow, and help isolate where things go wrong.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">process_data</span>(<span class="va">data</span>):
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"DEBUG: Input shape = </span>{<span class="va">data</span>.<span class="va">shape</span>}<span class="st">"</span>)
    <span class="va">normalized</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">data</span> <span class="op">/</span> <span class="va">data</span>.<span class="fn">max</span>()
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"DEBUG: Range = [</span>{<span class="va">normalized</span>.<span class="fn">min</span>()}<span class="st">, </span>{<span class="va">normalized</span>.<span class="fn">max</span>()}<span class="st">]"</span>)
    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="va">normalized</span>

<span class="cm"># Using Python Debugger (pdb)</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">pdb</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">train_model</span>(<span class="va">data</span>):
    <span class="nm">pdb</span>.<span class="fn">set_trace</span>()  <span class="cm"># Pauses execution here</span>
    <span class="va">weights</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">initialize_weights</span>()</span></code></pre>

  <h3>Assertions for Sanity Checks</h3>
  <p>Assertions verify conditions hold true. If they fail, execution stops immediately at the problem.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">calculate_accuracy</span>(<span class="va">predictions</span>, <span class="va">labels</span>):
    <span class="kw">assert</span> <span class="va">predictions</span>.<span class="va">shape</span> <span class="op">==</span> <span class="va">labels</span>.<span class="va">shape</span>, \
        <span class="st">"Predictions and labels must have same shape"</span>
    <span class="kw">assert</span> <span class="nm">0</span> <span class="op"><=</span> <span class="va">predictions</span>.<span class="fn">min</span>() <span class="op"><=</span> <span class="nm">1</span>, \
        <span class="st">"Predictions must be in [0,1]"</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> (<span class="va">predictions</span> <span class="op">==</span> <span class="va">labels</span>).<span class="fn">mean</span>()</span></code></pre>

  <!-- INFOGRAPHIC: Debugging Process -->
